Forking, a main process, and a background process are foundational concepts of how Linux manages programs, multitasking, and system resources.

2. Main Process (Foreground / Primary Process)

A main process (often referred to in the context of a foreground process or primary application loop) is a process that directly interacts with the user and ties up the terminal or shell from which it was launched.
• Behavior: When you run a standard command or application in a terminal (like vim, nano, or a python script), it runs in the foreground. It captures your keyboard input and sends its output (stdout/stderr) straight to your screen.
• Control: Your terminal prompt waits until the main process finishes or is stopped. You can suspend it using Ctrl + Z or terminate it using Ctrl + C.


3. Background Process

A background process is a task that runs independently of direct user interaction in the terminal.
• Behavior: It runs silently (or writes logs to a file/terminal without blocking your ability to type new commands).

• How to start one:
	• You can launch a command directly into the background by appending an ampersand (&) at the end of it 
    (e.g., long_script.sh &).

	• You can send an already running foreground process to the background by pressing Ctrl + Z to pause it, and then typing bg to resume it in the background.

• Managing them: 
   You can view active background jobs using the jobs command 
   bring any of them back to the foreground using fg [job_number]. 
   (Note: True system services that run detached from any terminal are a special class of background processes called daemons, which are often spawned via forking).

   A standard "background process" is simply any task running out of sight they use users permission who ran it
   (for instance, running a command with & at the end). 
   
A daemon is a specialized version. They traditionally run with system-level privileges, start automatically when the computer boots up, and remain dormant in the memory until a specific event triggers them to act.


